  "question": {
    "text": "For the equation \\(\\frac{1}{x+a}-\\frac{1}{x+b}=\\frac{1}{x+c}\\), if the product of roots is zero, then the sum of roots is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "0cxUo",
      "text": "0",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "sHjhS",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{2 \\mathrm{ab}}{\\mathrm{b}+\\mathrm{c}}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CAzLB",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{2 b c}{b+c}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "R1uLT",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{-2 b c}{b+c}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "\\(\\frac{1}{x+a}-\\frac{1}{x+b}=\\frac{1}{x+c}\\)\nor \\(x^2+(a+b) x+a b=(b-a) x+(b-a) c\\) or \\(\\mathrm{x}^2+2 \\mathrm{ax}+\\mathrm{ab}+\\mathrm{ca}-\\mathrm{bc}=0\\) Since product of the roots \\(=0\\)\n\\(a b+c a-b c=0 ; a=\\frac{b c}{b+c}\\)\nThus sum of roots \\(=-2 a=\\frac{-2 b c}{b+c}\\)",
    "image": null
  },
